{"kind":"artifact","text":"Corrected MCP protocol-era documentation, unmerged on branch task/docs-era at 1bc7220f53474f78ccffe3376c6d48264a73d858. Both pages told a new user that this build serves only the stateless 2026-07-28 shape and that a client opening with initialize cannot attach; that was true and deliberately written when written, and the dual-era merge falsified it. Both sections now say what a client should expect on each path rather than only that it works: a modern client carries per-request metadata and may probe with server/discover, and naming an unserved version returns -32022 listing what is served so it can retry; a legacy client opens with initialize and is answered in its own revision when the adapter speaks it, otherwise in 2025-11-25, which it may refuse. The reference carries this as a table of how the client opens against what the adapter answers, including the -32602 rejection of an incomplete handshake, which leaves the era undetermined so the client may open again. Both pages state that the opening settles the era once: initialize after modern traffic is refused, a second initialize is refused rather than renegotiating the version mid-stream, server/discover is unavailable on a legacy connection, and a refused opening leaves a working session working. Every claim was probed against the binary built from merged main rather than against the branch that introduced the behaviour, because a document describing a shipped adapter should be checked against the shipped adapter. The same commit narrows a second claim I had made too broadly: the reference said nothing on the write path checks whether a cited event resolves, and the truth is that the fold enforces the commitment chain, where a promise naming a nonexistent request is ineffective and its report ineffective in turn, but does not enforce evidential rests_on, where a dangling basis records as effective. The division is deliberate and a reader is better served by it than by the broad negative. 29 links and anchors across 20 files resolve; make vet, make test, make build pass and make ui rebuilds byte-identical output. Not reviewed and not merged.","body":{"commit":"1bc7220f53474f78ccffe3376c6d48264a73d858","path":"docs"}}